@*
	Ties a Home Assistant dropdown helper to the engine's period table, in whichever direction the household
	wants it to run.

	One helper, two entirely different jobs, and never both at once. Under Home Assistant's authority the
	dropdown is an INPUT: the household's own automations move it and every room runs whatever period it names,
	whatever the clock says. Under adaptive lighting's it is an OUTPUT: the engine keeps it pointing at the
	period its own schedule resolved, so a dashboard or a template elsewhere in the house can read the time of
	day the lights are actually running on.

	THE ROWS COME FROM THE HELPER, NOT FROM THE DOCUMENT. A mapping is only ever useful for an option the
	select actually offers, so the editor draws one row per live option and the document follows. A stored row
	whose value the helper no longer offers is the rename case — it is shown apart, named as such, and can be
	moved onto a live option in one pick rather than retyped. It is deliberately not deleted for you: the
	period it names is somebody's decision, and dropping it would make the warning the only trace it existed.

	A helper with more options than the schedule has periods cannot be fully mapped, so the shortfall is
	counted and the schedule's own "add a period" is offered here rather than described.
*@

<div class="periodselect">
	<div class="field">
		<div class="field-head">
			<label class="field-label">Dropdown helper
				<InfoPopover Label="Dropdown helper">
					Which <code>input_select</code> holds the time of day. No helper yet? Make one in Home Assistant
					under <strong>Settings &rarr; Devices &amp; services &rarr; Helpers &rarr; Create helper &rarr;
					Dropdown</strong>, give it one option per period, then pick it here.
				</InfoPopover>
			</label>
		</div>
		<div class="field-control">
			<EntityPicker Value="@Select?.Entity"
						  ValueChanged="@EntityChanged"
						  Options="@InputSelects"
						  NameResolver="@NameResolver"
						  KnownResolver="@KnownResolver"
						  NoneLabel="(none — the schedule decides the time of day)"
						  EmptyNote="@EmptyNote"
						  Placeholder="input_select.tid_pa_dagnet" />
		</div>
		<div class="field-help">
			Without one, periods change at the start times in the schedule above and nothing else is read or written.
		</div>
	</div>

	@if (HasEntity)
	{
		@*
			The one control that decides what everything below means, so it comes before the mappings rather
			than after them. Radios rather than a switch: the two directions are not on and off, they are two
			different jobs, and a switch would leave a reader guessing which way "on" ran.
		*@
		<div class="field">
			<div class="field-head">
				<span class="field-label">Changes to periods are set by:
					<InfoPopover Label="Changes to periods are set by">
						<strong>Home Assistant</strong> means the engine follows this dropdown: whatever option it
						stands on names the period every room runs, and the start times in the schedule stop
						deciding anything. <strong>Adaptive lighting</strong> means the engine decides from its own
						schedule and keeps the dropdown pointing at the answer, so the rest of your house can read it.
					</InfoPopover>
				</span>
			</div>
			<div class="field-control periodselect-authority">
				<label class="periodselect-choice">
					<input type="radio" name="period-authority"
						   checked="@(Authority == PeriodAuthority.HomeAssistant)"
						   @onchange="@(() => SetAuthority(PeriodAuthority.HomeAssistant))" />
					Home Assistant
				</label>
				<label class="periodselect-choice">
					<input type="radio" name="period-authority"
						   checked="@(Authority == PeriodAuthority.AdaptiveLighting)"
						   @onchange="@(() => SetAuthority(PeriodAuthority.AdaptiveLighting))" />
					Adaptive lighting
				</label>
			</div>
			<div class="field-help">@AuthorityHelp</div>
		</div>

		@*
			What is wrong with this pairing right now, said in the order it costs the household. The helper being
			unreadable outranks everything, because under Home Assistant's authority the whole house is back on
			the schedule until it answers; the current option not being mapped is next, because it is the one
			fault that is happening rather than waiting to.
		*@
		@if (!IsAvailable && IsReady)
		{
			<div class="notice bad periodselect-warning">
				<strong>Home Assistant doesn't know <code>@Select!.EntityId</code>.</strong>
				<span>@UnavailableNote</span>
			</div>
		}
		else if (CurrentIsUnmapped)
		{
			<div class="notice warn periodselect-warning">
				<strong>The helper is on &ldquo;@ActiveValue&rdquo;, and nothing maps it to a period.</strong>
				<span>@UnmappedNote</span>
			</div>
		}

		@if (Shortfall > 0)
		{
			<div class="notice periodselect-warning">
				<strong>@ShortfallTitle</strong>
				<span>
					Every option needs a period of its own &mdash; two options cannot share one, because the engine
					would have no way to tell them apart.
				</span>
				<div class="panel-actions">
					<button type="button" @onclick="AddPeriod">Add a period</button>
				</div>
			</div>
		}

		@if (_liveValues.Count == 0)
		{
			<div class="empty">
				@if (IsReady)
				{
					<text>This helper has no options yet. Add one option per period in Home Assistant, then come back here.</text>
				}
				else
				{
					<text>Waiting for Home Assistant to list the helper's options.</text>
				}
			</div>
		}
		else
		{
			<div class="periodselect-rows">
				@foreach (string live in _liveValues)
				{
					@* Captured per iteration: the handlers below outlive the loop, and a closure over the loop
					   variable would map every row to whichever option happened to be last. *@
					string option = live;
					PeriodSelectOptionConfig? row = Find(option);
					string chosen = row?.Period ?? string.Empty;

					<div class="periodselect-row" @key="option">
						<span class="periodselect-option">
							<code>@option</code>
							@if (IsActive(option))
							{
								<span class="badge active-now" title="The helper is standing on this option right now.">now</span>
							}
						</span>

						<select class="cell wide" @bind:get="chosen" @bind:set="@(value => SetPeriod(option, value))">
							@* Never opens with the dash: a leading em dash reads as a separator rather than as the
							   option that is currently chosen. The same "(none — …)" form the rest of the page uses. *@
							<option value="">(none — this option changes nothing)</option>
							@foreach (string name in PeriodChoices(chosen))
							{
								<option value="@name">@name</option>
							}
							@if (IsDeadPeriod(chosen))
							{
								<option value="@chosen">@chosen — no such period</option>
							}
						</select>
					</div>
				}
			</div>
		}

		@if (Orphans.Count > 0)
		{
			<div class="periodselect-orphans">
				@foreach (PeriodSelectOptionConfig stale in Orphans)
				{
					PeriodSelectOptionConfig orphan = stale;

					<div class="periodselect-orphan" @key="orphan">
						<span class="badge bad" title="This value is no longer one of the helper's options.">renamed in Home Assistant</span>
						<span class="periodselect-orphan-text">
							<code>@orphan.Value</code> means <b>@OrphanPeriod(orphan)</b>, and the helper no longer offers it.
						</span>

						@if (UnmappedLiveValues.Count > 0)
						{
							<select class="cell" value="" @onchange="@(args => Remap(orphan, args.Value as string))">
								<option value="">move it to&hellip;</option>
								@foreach (string free in UnmappedLiveValues)
								{
									<option value="@free">@free</option>
								}
							</select>
						}

						<button type="button" class="danger" @onclick="@(() => Forget(orphan))">Remove</button>
					</div>
				}
			</div>
		}
	}
</div>

@code {
	/// <summary>The house settings, which own <see cref="GlobalConfig.PeriodSelect"/> and are edited in place.</summary>
	[Parameter, EditorRequired]
	public GlobalConfig Global { get; set; } = new();

	/// <summary>
	///     The document's period list, for the mappings' dropdowns and for the "add a period" shortfall button.
	/// </summary>
	/// <remarks>
	///     Edited in place, exactly as <see cref="PeriodsEditor"/> edits it, and only ever by appending the one
	///     row <see cref="PeriodsEditor.NewPeriod"/> defines — so the period a household adds from here and the
	///     period it adds from the table below are the same period, with the same defaults.
	/// </remarks>
	[Parameter, EditorRequired]
	public List<TimePeriodConfig> Periods { get; set; } = [];

	/// <summary>The <c>input_select</c> entities the helper picker offers.</summary>
	[Parameter]
	public IReadOnlyList<EntityOption> InputSelects { get; set; } = [];

	/// <summary>
	///     The helper's live options, as Home Assistant reports them. Empty means Home Assistant has not
	///     answered, never "the helper has no options" — see <see cref="IsReady"/>.
	/// </summary>
	[Parameter]
	public IReadOnlyList<string> LiveOptions { get; set; } = [];

	/// <summary>The helper's current option, or <c>null</c> when it is unknown or unavailable.</summary>
	[Parameter]
	public string? ActiveValue { get; set; }

	/// <summary>Whether Home Assistant knows the configured entity at all.</summary>
	[Parameter]
	public bool IsAvailable { get; set; }

	/// <summary>
	///     Whether Home Assistant is actually answering. The page knows this; this component uses it so an empty
	///     option list stops hedging and says the honest thing instead.
	/// </summary>
	[Parameter]
	public bool IsReady { get; set; } = true;

	/// <summary>What the helper picker says when it has nothing to offer, in the page's own words.</summary>
	[Parameter]
	public string EmptyNote { get; set; } = "Nothing to pick from yet — type an id instead.";

	/// <summary>Resolves an id to a friendly name for the helper picker. Optional.</summary>
	[Parameter]
	public Func<string, string>? NameResolver { get; set; }

	/// <summary>Whether Home Assistant knows an id, for the picker's "unknown" styling. Optional.</summary>
	[Parameter]
	public Func<string, bool>? KnownResolver { get; set; }

	/// <summary>
	///     Raised with the chosen entity id, or <c>null</c> for none.
	/// </summary>
	/// <remarks>
	///     The entity is the one field this component does <b>not</b> write itself. Changing it changes which
	///     helper's live options and current value the rows are drawn from, and only the page can re-read those —
	///     so the page owns the write, exactly as it owns the house-mode helper's.
	/// </remarks>
	[Parameter]
	public EventCallback<string?> EntityChanged { get; set; }

	/// <summary>Raised after any change this component made, so the page can re-validate.</summary>
	[Parameter]
	public EventCallback Changed { get; set; }

	/// <summary>The configured block, or <c>null</c> before a helper has ever been picked.</summary>
	private PeriodSelectConfig? Select => Global.PeriodSelect;

	/// <summary>Whether a helper is named, which is what every control below the picker depends on.</summary>
	/// <remarks>
	///     The entity, not the block: <c>ConfigNormalizer</c> keeps an entity-less block only until the next save,
	///     and <c>PeriodSelectReader.For</c> builds nothing without an entity — so an authority radio over a blank
	///     entity would be a control with no effect at all.
	/// </remarks>
	private bool HasEntity => Select?.EntityId is not null;

	/// <summary>Which side currently owns the time of day.</summary>
	private PeriodAuthority Authority => Select?.Authority ?? PeriodAuthority.AdaptiveLighting;

	/// <summary>The stored mappings, never <c>null</c> — a block that does not exist yet has none.</summary>
	private IReadOnlyList<PeriodSelectOptionConfig> Options => Select?.Options ?? [];

	/// <summary>The helper's live options, blank-free and de-duplicated the way every value here is compared.</summary>
	/// <remarks>
	///     <para>
	///         Resolved once per parameter set rather than on each read. It was a property running a nested-loop
	///         de-dupe, and the markup reaches it a dozen times per render — directly, and again through
	///         <see cref="Shortfall"/>, <see cref="Orphans"/> and <see cref="UnmappedLiveValues"/>, the last of which
	///         is read twice inside the orphan loop without depending on the orphan.
	///     </para>
	///     <para>
	///         Safe to cache because it is a pure function of the <see cref="LiveOptions"/> parameter. The lists that
	///         read <i>it</i> stay properties, because they also read <see cref="Options"/>, which this component
	///         mutates between renders — caching those would serve a stale table after an edit.
	///     </para>
	/// </remarks>
	private List<string> _liveValues = [];

	/// <inheritdoc/>
	protected override void OnParametersSet() => _liveValues = HouseModeSync.Clean(LiveOptions);

	/// <summary>Live options no mapping names yet — what an orphan can be moved onto in one pick.</summary>
	private List<string> UnmappedLiveValues =>
		[.. _liveValues.Where(value => Find(value) is null)];

	/// <summary>
	///     Stored mappings the helper no longer offers: the rename case.
	/// </summary>
	/// <remarks>
	///     Empty whenever the question cannot be asked. An empty <see cref="LiveOptions"/> is a Home Assistant
	///     that has not answered, not a helper with no options — read as an answer it would condemn every mapping
	///     at once, striking through the whole table the moment the connection blinked. The same refusal
	///     <c>HouseModeOptions.IsLive</c> makes, for the same reason.
	/// </remarks>
	private List<PeriodSelectOptionConfig> Orphans
	{
		get
		{
			List<string> live = _liveValues;

			return live.Count == 0
				? []
				: [.. Options.Where(option =>
					!string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(option.Value)
					&& !live.Any(value => Eq(value, option.Value)))];
		}
	}

	/// <summary>Whether the helper's current option maps to nothing, which is the one fault that is happening now.</summary>
	/// <remarks>
	///     Only asked under Home Assistant's authority. Under adaptive lighting's the engine <i>writes</i> this
	///     helper, so an unmapped value is a helper somebody moved by hand and the engine is about to correct —
	///     which is not a fault, and saying it was would send somebody to fix a mapping they do not need.
	/// </remarks>
	private bool CurrentIsUnmapped =>
		Authority is PeriodAuthority.HomeAssistant
		&& ActiveValue is { Length: > 0 }
		&& Select?.PeriodFor(ActiveValue) is not { Length: > 0 };

	/// <summary>How many live options cannot be mapped, because the schedule has fewer periods than the helper has options.</summary>
	private int Shortfall => Math.Max(0, _liveValues.Count - Periods.Count);

	/// <summary>Whether the helper stands on this option right now.</summary>
	private bool IsActive(string value) => ActiveValue is { Length: > 0 } && Eq(ActiveValue, value);

	/// <summary>The mapping for a live option, or <c>null</c> when nothing names it.</summary>
	private PeriodSelectOptionConfig? Find(string value) =>
		Options.FirstOrDefault(option => Eq(option.Value, value));

	/// <summary>What an orphan's period is called, or a placeholder for a row that named none.</summary>
	private static string OrphanPeriod(PeriodSelectOptionConfig orphan) =>
		orphan.Period is { Length: > 0 } period ? period : "no period";

	/// <summary>
	///     The periods a row may choose: the ones nothing else has taken, plus whatever this row already holds.
	/// </summary>
	/// <remarks>
	///     Two options mapping to one period is an ambiguity the engine cannot resolve in the writing direction —
	///     <see cref="PeriodSelectConfig.OptionFor"/> takes the first — so the list simply does not offer it. The
	///     row's own choice stays on the list regardless, because a dropdown that hid its current value would
	///     make remapping the one thing this editor could not do.
	/// </remarks>
	private List<string> PeriodChoices(string chosen)
	{
		List<string> taken = [.. Options
			.Select(option => option.Period)
			.Where(period => !string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(period) && !Eq(period, chosen))];

		return
		[
			.. Periods
				.Select(period => period.Name)
				.Where(name => !string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(name))
				.Where(name => !taken.Any(other => NamesPeriod(name, other)))
		];
	}

	/// <summary>
	///     Whether a mapping's stored period text names the schedule period called <paramref name="periodName"/>.
	/// </summary>
	/// <remarks>
	///     <b>Trims the mapping's side only, because that is what the document does.</b>
	///     <see cref="ConfigValidator"/>, <see cref="Schedule.NamedBySelect"/> and
	///     <c>CircadianCalculator.OverriddenPeriod</c> all match a trimmed <c>Option.Period</c> against a raw
	///     <c>TimePeriodConfig.Name</c>. This editor used its own <see cref="Eq"/>, which trims both — so a period
	///     named <c>"night "</c> mapped to <c>"night"</c> read as an ordinary valid choice here while the validator
	///     refused the whole document and the engine quietly fell back to the clock. An editor that disagrees with
	///     the validator about what it just accepted is worse than one that offers less.
	/// </remarks>
	/// <param name="periodName">A period's name, exactly as the schedule carries it.</param>
	/// <param name="chosen">A mapping's stored period text.</param>
	private static bool NamesPeriod(string? periodName, string? chosen) =>
		string.Equals(periodName, chosen?.Trim(), StringComparison.OrdinalIgnoreCase);

	/// <summary>Whether a stored choice names a period the schedule no longer has, so the dropdown must still show it.</summary>
	/// <remarks>
	///     Kept visible rather than silently blanked: the validator refuses the document over exactly this, and a
	///     dropdown that had quietly reset itself would leave the refusal with nothing on screen to explain it.
	/// </remarks>
	private bool IsDeadPeriod(string chosen) =>
		chosen is { Length: > 0 } && !Periods.Any(period => NamesPeriod(period.Name, chosen));

	/// <summary>What the radio's help line says, which is a different sentence for each direction.</summary>
	private string AuthorityHelp => Authority is PeriodAuthority.HomeAssistant
		? "The engine reads this helper and never writes it. Start times in the schedule above are kept, but they "
			+ "stop deciding anything until you hand the time of day back."
		: "The engine writes this helper to match whatever its schedule resolved. Moving the helper by hand changes "
			+ "nothing — it is put back on the next re-check.";

	/// <summary>Why an unknown entity is worth saying out loud, in the terms of the direction it breaks.</summary>
	private string UnavailableNote => Authority is PeriodAuthority.HomeAssistant
		? "Until it appears, every room follows the schedule's start times instead. Check the id for a typo."
		: "Until it appears, the engine has nothing to write the time of day to. Check the id for a typo.";

	/// <summary>What an unmapped current option costs, which is the whole house for as long as it stands there.</summary>
	private string UnmappedNote =>
		"Every room is following the schedule's start times until it does. Give this option a period below, or pick "
		+ "a different one in Home Assistant.";

	/// <summary>The shortfall's own sentence, counted rather than described.</summary>
	private string ShortfallTitle
	{
		get
		{
			int options = _liveValues.Count;
			int periods = Periods.Count;
			string shortfall = Shortfall == 1 ? "one of them has" : $"{Shortfall} of them have";

			return $"This helper has {options} {(options == 1 ? "option" : "options")} and the schedule has "
				+ $"{periods} {(periods == 1 ? "period" : "periods")}, so {shortfall} no period to point at.";
		}
	}

	/// <summary>
	///     Sets which side owns the time of day.
	/// </summary>
	/// <remarks>
	///     The mappings are untouched by the switch, because they mean something in both directions: one reads
	///     them option-to-period, the other period-to-option. Clearing them on a switch would make trying the
	///     other direction cost the whole table.
	/// </remarks>
	private Task SetAuthority(PeriodAuthority authority) => Set(() => Block().Authority = authority);

	/// <summary>Points a live option at a period, or clears it.</summary>
	/// <remarks>
	///     <b>A cleared row is REMOVED, not emptied.</b> Emptying only the period looked tidier and was a trap:
	///     <c>PeriodSelectOptionConfig.IsEmpty</c> is <c>Value</c> blank <b>and</b> <c>Period</c> blank, so a row
	///     still carrying its option string is not empty, the normaliser keeps it, and the validator then raises a
	///     document-level <i>error</i> — which <c>LightingEngineHost.Save</c> refuses on. Picking "this option
	///     changes nothing", an option this editor offers as legitimate, would have blocked every save on the whole
	///     Configuration page, including unrelated room edits. Worse, clearing the helper to back out hides this
	///     panel behind <c>HasEntity</c> while the error stays, leaving no control on screen able to undo it: a
	///     hand edit of the YAML was the only way out.
	///     <para>
	///         Nothing is lost by removing it. The option is still live on the helper, so it renders as an
	///         unmapped row and is one pick away from being mapped again.
	///     </para>
	/// </remarks>
	private Task SetPeriod(string value, string? period) => Set(() =>
	{
		PeriodSelectOptionConfig? existing = Find(value);

		if (string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(period))
		{
			if (existing is not null)
				Block().Options.Remove(existing);

			return;
		}

		if (existing is null)
			Block().Options.Add(new PeriodSelectOptionConfig { Value = value, Period = period.Trim() });
		else
			existing.Period = period.Trim();
	});

	/// <summary>
	///     Moves a stale mapping onto a live option, keeping the period it named.
	/// </summary>
	/// <remarks>
	///     The rename case, answered in one pick. Rewriting the value rather than adding a row and deleting the
	///     old one is what keeps the period: a household that renamed <c>Kveld</c> to <c>Evening</c> in Home
	///     Assistant did not change their mind about what it means.
	/// </remarks>
	private Task Remap(PeriodSelectOptionConfig orphan, string? live) =>
		string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(live) ? Task.CompletedTask : Set(() => orphan.Value = live.Trim());

	/// <summary>Drops a stale mapping outright, for the option that was removed rather than renamed.</summary>
	private Task Forget(PeriodSelectOptionConfig orphan) => Set(() => Block().Options.Remove(orphan));

	/// <summary>
	///     Appends a period, so a helper with more options than periods can be finished without leaving here.
	/// </summary>
	/// <remarks>
	///     The row is <see cref="PeriodsEditor.NewPeriod"/>'s, not one built here, so the two places a household
	///     can add a period cannot disagree about what a new one starts as.
	/// </remarks>
	private Task AddPeriod() => Set(() => Periods.Add(PeriodsEditor.NewPeriod(Periods)));

	/// <summary>
	///     The block to write into, created on first use.
	/// </summary>
	/// <remarks>
	///     Every control that calls this is behind <see cref="HasEntity"/>, so in practice the block already
	///     exists — but binding it into existence is the documented contract with <c>ConfigNormalizer</c>, which
	///     drops a block that ends up saying nothing rather than leaving a <c>PeriodSelect:</c> stanza in the file
	///     of a household that never adopted the feature.
	/// </remarks>
	private PeriodSelectConfig Block() => Global.PeriodSelect ??= new PeriodSelectConfig();

	private Task Set(Action mutate)
	{
		mutate();
		return Changed.InvokeAsync();
	}

	/// <summary>
	///     How two option strings are compared, everywhere on this panel.
	/// </summary>
	/// <remarks>
	///     Trimmed and case-insensitive, matching <see cref="PeriodSelectConfig.PeriodFor"/> exactly. These are
	///     display strings a person typed into a Home Assistant helper, and an editor stricter than the engine
	///     would draw an "unmapped" row for a mapping the engine is happily reading.
	/// </remarks>
	private static bool Eq(string? left, string? right) =>
		string.Equals(left?.Trim(), right?.Trim(), StringComparison.OrdinalIgnoreCase);
}
